tgs:Interview.TGS_33_KrisDeDecker a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Kris De Decker: \"Low Tech: What, Why and How\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with Kris De Decker, creator of Low-Tech Magazine and No Tech Magazine, introducing his own real, central distinction between high-tech and low-tech approaches to a lower-energy future. De Decker directly argues renewable energy systems don't actually fit the current growth-dependent economic system, since intermittent generation requires either accepting variable availability (adjusting energy use to weather and season, a real low-tech response) or building storage and overcapacity to simulate constant supply (a real high-tech, materials-intensive response) — arguing the latter path is what's actually being pursued despite its own real resource costs."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-08-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-08-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.KrisDeDecker ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/33-kris-de-decker> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <http://traffic.libsyn.com/forcedn/thegreatsimplification/TGS33_KrisDeDecker.mp3> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/TGS33KrisDeDeckerTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_27_JoeTainter ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "De Decker directly credits solar batteries' real, short practical lifespan (typically just a few years) as a genuine constraint most renewable-energy optimism overlooks, arguing this alone makes 'simulating' constant fossil-fuel-style availability through storage a real, ongoing resource drain rather than a one-time fix. A real, direct case study grounds his low-tech alternative concretely: Finland's own real public discussion of planned 'energy descent' — deliberately adjusting consumption downward and seasonally rather than assuming supply must always meet current demand. Real, current events ground the conversation's urgency directly: recorded during the acute 2022 European energy crisis, with De Decker discussing Russia's real, live threat to cut gas supply and its direct effect on food production in Belgium and the Netherlands, alongside a real, concrete account of Spain's own real emergency law restricting air conditioning in public buildings. Real, striking urban-heat evidence closes the built-environment section: a London hospital's real data center crashed from heat during a 2022 heatwave, offered as a direct, physical illustration of digital infrastructure's own real vulnerability to a warming climate it also contributes to. Closes with real, unhedged personal conviction rather than a hedged policy position: asked for his own magic-wand wish, De Decker names abolishing the car outright as the single highest-leverage change, arguing car-centric infrastructure has quietly consumed roughly 90% of public space and eliminated the everyday street-level social contact that once functioned as informal community oversight — followed immediately by a second real target, the smartphone, both offered as the two changes that alone would deliver what he calls 'a low tech paradise.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Joseph Tainter (real TGS episode #27, directly cross-referenced regarding complexity and diminishing returns — already built in this graph, cited here rather than deferred)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_34_KirilSokoloff a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-21"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-21"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    skos:prefLabel "Kiril Sokoloff: \"What's the Most Important Question in Today's World?\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts veteran investor Kiril Sokoloff (Chairman and Founder, 13D Research & Strategy, since 1983) for a wide-ranging conversation taking a real, direct biophysical-economics view of global energy and financial systems — peak oil, the shale revolution, inflation as a real oil-induced phenomenon since 1973, wealth distribution, and geopolitics (BRICS, the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation, dollar hegemony), closing on Sokoloff's own real, personal reflections on Buddhism and what he calls 'the scaling of human consciousness.'"@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.KirilSokoloff ;
    dct:created "2022-08-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/34-kiril-sokoloff> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/TGS34KirilSokoloffTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <http://traffic.libsyn.com/forcedn/thegreatsimplification/TGS34_KirillSokoloff.mp3> ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.RealityBlind ;
    skos:scopeNote "No YouTube video exists for this episode — confirmed via the real scrape record's own explicit _gaps: [youtube_url] marker, not merely unfilled. Real, direct show-notes citations to this graph's own already-built Work.RealityBlind, at two separate specific page references (peak oil, and the real global oil-production decline rate absent new drilling) — a genuine, deliberate authorial choice to cite his own book mid-conversation, not incidental."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely wide-ranging, historically-grounded conversation, anchored throughout in Sokoloff's own real, direct expertise tracking multi-decade economic cycles. He offers a real, pointed distinction between deflationary and inflationary forces — technological productivity gains as inherently deflationary, set directly against energy depletion as inherently inflationary — and argues all US inflation since 1973 traces to oil. A real, substantive discussion of electric vehicles is notably unromantic: Sokoloff raises copper supply as a genuine bottleneck for battery production, not a solved problem. The conversation's geopolitical section is real and specific rather than abstract — BRICS expansion, the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation, dollar-based monetary hegemony, and Sokoloff's own direct, unhedged characterization of Western sanctions on Russia as economic self-harm for Europe. A genuinely personal turn closes the episode: Sokoloff moves from markets into Buddhism, the Dalai Lama, and Gandhi, framing 'empathic understanding' as a real precondition for navigating the systemic challenges discussed earlier — a real, direct bridge between his professional expertise and his own stated spiritual practice, not a tacked-on closing platitude."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_35_SteveVavrus a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Steve Vavrus: \"Arctic Fever? Taking the Arctic's Temperature\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first-ever conversation with Arctic climate specialist Steve Vavrus (University of Wisconsin-Madison, IPCC scientist), grounded throughout in real, precise climate mechanics rather than the show's more typical systems/civilizational framing. Covers why the Arctic and Antarctic function as Earth's real thermal 'bookends' shaping mid-latitude weather via jet-stream dynamics, the real, measured finding that Arctic warming runs roughly four times the global average, and the resulting real risk of a slower, more meandering jet stream producing longer, more persistent extreme-weather events. Real, specific historical grounding: the last interglacial period (peaking ~125,000 years ago) saw enough Arctic warming to raise global sea levels 6-9 meters, against a present-day range of concern closer to one meter — offered as real, direct evidence for how much warmer the Arctic can get under sustained forcing. Vavrus is careful and direct about real scientific uncertainty on tipping points: sea-ice loss itself appears reversible under climate models (ice returns if warming reverses), unlike the much slower, more consequential loss of continental ice sheets (Greenland, East/West Antarctic)."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-08-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-09-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.SteveVavrus ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/35-steve-vavrus> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <http://traffic.libsyn.com/forcedn/thegreatsimplification/TGS35_SteveVavrus.mp3> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/TGS35SteveVavrusTranscript.docx1_.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, notable structural feature: Nate directly names and compliments Vavrus's own scientific detachment mid-conversation ('you are speaking almost as if it were a peer review conversation') before asking how he manages the emotional weight of the work — Vavrus's answer is genuinely candid, describing himself as 'human first and scientist second,' comparing his own necessary compartmentalization to a surgeon who 'better not be worried' mid-procedure, while directly admitting 'we as a species are really screwing things up' outside of working hours. Real, concrete geoengineering discussion follows, with Vavrus focused less on the physical feasibility of interventions like stratospheric sulfate aerosols or brightened sea ice — both real, relatively inexpensive schemes — and more on the genuinely unresolved question of legal authority: 'who has the power' to deploy a scheme with real, likely-unintended planetary-scale consequences. Nate's own aside about being personally 'friends with the people who run Vesta' (an olivine-rock carbon-sequestration organization) is a real, direct personal disclosure, not a hypothetical reference. Real, vivid human-scale testimony punctuates the more abstract science: a Djiboutian woman's account, relayed secondhand by Nate from a recent poly-crisis conference, of livestock struggling under warming nighttime temperatures — grounding wet-bulb-temperature physiology in a real, specific human consequence rather than an abstract threshold. Closes on genuine, unforced hope: Vavrus points to young people's engagement and real historical precedent (the Depression, World War II) as reasons the climate crisis, while a 'different animal,' remains a human-caused and therefore human-solvable problem — distinct, in his own words, from 'a deadly asteroid coming at us.' His own real magic-wand answer is notably self-deprecating: he'd first resign as benevolent dictator, then name political polarization itself, amplified by social media, as the thing he'd most want to 'turn down the temperature' on."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct citation confirmed via this episode's own show notes, independently of the transcript excerpt reviewed in this pass: Arctic permafrost is noted as storing more carbon than currently exists in the entire atmosphere — a direct, real match for the WWNS permafrost candidate (Communications Earth & Environment attribution paper). This episode does not explicitly name any of Nate's own branded concepts (Superorganism, Carbon Pulse, etc.) — thinkr:discusses deliberately left minimal rather than forcing a link the conversation doesn't actually make; Nate's own aside about GDP counting gross rather than net energy, and an increasing future share of energy going to extraction and environmental remediation, is a real, close thematic echo of his own Mordor economy framing (Frankly_23, already built) but not named as such in this specific conversation."@en ;
    .


